{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  "name": "zenboard-web",
  "compatibility_date": "2024-12-30",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },
  "images": {
    "binding": "IMAGES"
  },
  // ── THE FREE INFERENCE WE ALREADY OWN (2026-09-27) ────────────────────────
  //
  // Workers AI gives this account 10,000 Neurons a DAY, free, for ever, on the plan Zenboard is
  // already on. No second vendor, no key to rotate, no card. It is the only free option that needs
  // no hardware: Ollama wants a machine running all day and the leaderboard ASR models (Canary,
  // Parakeet, Qwen3-ASR) want a GPU — "free to self-host" quietly means "free if you own a GPU".
  //
  // WHAT THE BUDGET ACTUALLY BUYS, and why it decides the architecture:
  //   · Whisper is 41.14 neurons per AUDIO MINUTE → ~243 minutes a day, across every user. Eight
  //     half-hour meetings. Hopeless for 5,000 people.
  //   · A 30-minute meeting is only ~5,000 TOKENS of transcript. Llama 3.2 1B reads that for ~12
  //     neurons → ~800 meetings a day. Comfortable.
  // Transcribing costs a hundred times what thinking costs. So audio → text happens on the
  // visitor's own device (Whisper via transformers.js: free, unlimited, and the recording never
  // leaves their laptop, which is the honest answer for a call under an NDA), and text → notes
  // happens here. Groq's free tier (8 audio-hours a day) is the overflow valve, not the floor.
  "ai": {
    "binding": "AI"
  },
  // Run the Worker next to the database, not next to the visitor.
  //
  // Supabase (hctwmcqftwpefnisrhac) is in AWS ap-northeast-2, Seoul. Without
  // this the Worker answers from the Cloudflare edge nearest the visitor
  // (Singapore, for India) and every database query crosses to Seoul and back:
  // measured at 90-240 ms each, up to seven in series per page. Placed in Seoul,
  // a query costs a few ms and the visitor's request makes that trip once.
  // Static assets are unaffected: Cloudflare always serves them from the edge
  // nearest the visitor. Once live, responses carry `cf-placement: remote-…`.
  "placement": {
    "region": "aws:ap-northeast-2"
  },

  // ── LIVE ON zenboard.life ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Workers custom domains require the zone to live on Cloudflare; there is no
  // way to point a CNAME at workers.dev and have TLS work. So the nameservers
  // moved to Cloudflare at the registrar (Hostinger), and the .life registry
  // has delegated to them since 2026-09-28. There were no MX records, so the
  // move broke no email.
  //
  // With `custom_domain: true` wrangler creates the DNS record AND the
  // certificate itself. Nothing to add by hand in the Cloudflare DNS tab.
  //
  // `workers_dev` MUST stay spelled out. Wrangler's default for it is "on only
  // when there are no routes" (`getSubdomainValues`), so these two routes
  // would silently switch the workers.dev address OFF — and calendar feed
  // subscriptions, MCP clients, links in emails already sent and the smoke
  // probe in `npm run ship` all live on that address.
  "workers_dev": true,
  "routes": [
    { "pattern": "zenboard.life", "custom_domain": true },
    { "pattern": "www.zenboard.life", "custom_domain": true }
  ]
}
